The shared host, normal public navigation and normal loopback API are used unchanged. The browser clicks the existing visible controls and receives actual API responses. The spec does not inject a substitute client, handler, storage adapter or response body. The future hosted collector must additionally authenticate the complete eligible active source census, Git baseline outside these exact targets, source captures before execution and source stability afterward. The spec's eight-file preflight does not replace that collector proof.

The final Archive installation is being integrated separately. No generic or browser collector pin is frozen by this proposal. After the Archive commit, tooling authoring must read the actual new baseline, retain its installed Archive scope, and introduce a distinct exact Notes source experiment or installed-source scope. A stale shared collector must never be copied over the newer one. If Notes remains uninstalled, the existing reviewed Notes composer must perform its exact eight-target composition in the ephemeral hosted checkout, with an independent always-run postcheck. The historical Notes declarations remain uninstalled declarations.

## Eight scenarios

1. **Ordinary lifecycle and combined filters.** Create two notes through the real UI. Convert one, set millisecond due metadata, complete and reopen it, observe all five task filters, combine task state with search/tag/notebook, clear due metadata and convert back. Compare actual saved identity/title/body/notebook/tags and final task fields. Due metadata does not deliver an alarm.
2. **Dirty draft refuses transport.** Type ordinary newer title/body/tags in enabled fields, then try completion, due editing and conversion. Require the client message, zero additional task-mutation requests, exact draft fields and byte-identical actual stored record.
3. **Held actual commit and newer draft.** Fetch the actual successful completion response through Playwright routing, then hold publication. Independently read the actual disk record and require the committed revision/completion before typing. Type ordinarily in enabled title/body fields. Release the original response unchanged, require the newer draft, then Save through the UI and inspect its submitted revision and actual disk result. The completion button remains disabled while pending; the test does not bypass it.
4. **New note owns its editor.** Hold an actual committed completion response, click New note, type a new draft and release the original response. Require retained new fields/status and a separately saved new identity at revision one, while the original task's commit remains stored.
5. **Navigation disposes the old editor.** Hold an actual committed completion response, navigate using the real Lab Flow button and observe the old control is detached. Release the response and require no additional Notes requests or return to the old view. The saved task remains committed. Notes does not currently scrub detached control values; this scenario makes no scrubbing claim.
6. **Genuine conflict and explicit retry.** A separate owned HTTP actor actually saves a newer revision, leaving the browser stale. The browser's completion request must receive the real host's exact HTTP 400 revision error. Require unchanged disk bytes, restored controls and retained stale editor. Explicitly refresh/reopen the current note through the UI, then retry and require one new committed revision. No status/body is manufactured and no automatic retry is accepted.
7. **Typing during post-accept list refresh.** Hold the real list response requested after the actual completion has committed and its result has been accepted by the client. Type a newer body. Release the unchanged response, await the following actual notebook-list response and an animation-frame observation barrier, and require the unsaved-draft message and fields. Save must submit the accepted committed revision.
8. **Real JEX and resource downloads.** Import the exact public original-serialized fixture through the browser file control, edit task fields, and download JEX using the actual product download button. Parse/list the downloaded file through the host's independent native `System32/tar.exe`; require eight entries, all six untouched raw records' original hashes, exact edited note content/unrelated metadata, native IDs/relationships and the edited task fields. Download the linked resource through its actual button and compare exact bytes `[0,1,127,128,255]` with the TAR resource and original fixture digest. Both raw browser files are retained.

## Real timing and transport observations

Exactly four scenarios use one real response hold each: completion in cases three/four/five and list in case seven. A hold calls `route.fetch`, retains the actual successful response identity, and fulfills with the original response after release. Cases three/four/five independently check disk commit before proceeding; case seven checks the committed note in the actual list plus actual disk. Ordinary typing uses Playwright field filling on enabled inputs. Programmatic disabled-control probes are zero and must remain a separate category if ever added.

The genuine conflict is one explicitly registered actual HTTP 400 response with the exact expected revision error. Other failed API responses, request failures and page errors fail acceptance. Its browser console message is retained and qualified only for that observed exact endpoint/status; additional matching console errors still fail. The unchanged host's exact favicon 404 may be retained separately as an auxiliary resource observation. Raw request bodies contain only synthetic notes/public fixture input. Request headers/session tokens are not logged, and this dedicated configuration disables traces and video to avoid retaining those headers indirectly.

Each test owns a fresh data directory and loopback server. Cleanup releases and finishes held routes, captures a screenshot, drains owned page requests/response observers, preserves the synthetic final note record, closes its browser context/API pool, then closes the server. Individual cleanup deadlines are five seconds. Failed cleanup remains a failure with staged timestamps; aborted requests are not relabeled successful. Data directories are not recursively deleted by this spec.

## Runtime, files and acceptance

The dedicated configuration selects one worker, zero retries, `forbidOnly`, eight declared scenarios, a 60-second per-test deadline and ten-minute global deadline. It requires GitHub-hosted Windows and Node `24.16.0`. It uses the existing exact Playwright `1.64.1` toolchain and Chromium `156.0.8078.4`, with UTC timezone and real downloads enabled. No local browser installation or execution is authorized by this source proposal.

Every case retains one screenshot and case JSON, raw bounded synthetic request bodies, actual API response statuses, real hold identities, actual stored record identity, cleanup stages, errors and claimed downloads. The final case additionally retains the actual `.jex`, `.bin`, native TAR listing and parser executable identity. Screenshots and downloads must be nonempty/readable and match their recorded SHA-256 identities. A replacement browser worker writes a distinct process observation, authenticates the same browser and does not overwrite a prior observation. Eight accepted results require eight passes on retry zero, no skips/did-not-run/cancellations/flaky/retry acceptance, all cleanup flags true, expected four hold observations, two genuine file downloads and the actual conflict evidence. Any earlier failed run remains separately preserved.

The future hosted wrapper must preserve the complete actual source/census/attempt and browser report, including failures, under explicit existing storage limits. An oversize fallback must say incomplete and cannot certify a complete backup. Portable artifacts must be downloaded, hashes/ZIP CRCs checked, unpacked without executing contents and preserved locally in full. Only independently reviewed synthetic evidence may be published.

## Original-product boundary and remaining work

The fixture is 9,728 bytes, SHA-256 `044885c906ba7d8cb97a95fbdd6bc2323ba31a3c301e7ed1d6e592acec8944af`. Its 6,174-byte provenance has SHA-256 `cabc16edfc51e2e58fa0f147f31f5b5b54345a49597a3567583c3623d0fad220`. It was serialized from selected original helpers at source `c17c205049ce74e449dc1ce08cbd1c875410d7d5`, with the original documented date/database stubs. It is separate from the released Joplin 3.6.14 task helpers used by the earlier 20 comparisons. This browser suite imports no original executable or helper and performs zero new original-product executions. Native TAR is an independent archive parser, not a new Joplin run.

Independent source review, hosted tooling authoring against the final new baseline, actual hosted execution, complete backup/public evidence filing and later Notes installation remain pending. Notifications, new task draft workflow, original ordering/search grammar/sync and all other documented requirements remain open. These finite cases establish no full feature parity, security audit, comparative speed/resource improvement or universal superiority.

## Hosted tooling source proposal

The separate hosted tooling proposal now uses the exact post-Archive browser collector predecessor, 36,709 bytes / `558344c417dcd2178b406dce1bc9f96d27083429f75fc816683b2008aed88074`. Its existing Flow/Vault twelve-case body, experimental Archive body and installed Archive six-case body are retained. A new explicit `notes-todo-experiment` branch calls a closed Notes helper; the generic development collector is not a target. This remains source authorship, not execution acceptance.

The workflow first acquires the existing integrity-locked Playwright/Chromium tooling on a clean committed checkout. It then calls the unchanged exact Notes eight-target composer, runs the new browser scope and always attempts both the independent composer postcheck and separate browser full-source postcheck after any started composition. The Notes branch captures the complete eligible active `apps`, `lib`, `public`, `browser-tests`, `scripts` and `tests` directories plus the root server/catalog/package files under the original 2,000-file / 64 MiB / 8 MiB individual source limits. Every one of the seven eligible Notes after-images must match its fixed bundle; README is separately required. Every other captured source must authenticate as an ordinary committed Git blob. Both whole census and exact working state must remain stable. Root proof tools outside Studio are not newly claimed as browser-census coverage.

The closed helper authenticates the existing Notes bundle and all seventeen payloads, its three exact committed before-images and five absent additions, all eight actual current after-images, captured composer before/after bytes, same-attempt identity, browser spec/config/roster, workflow and source contract. It reads committed before-images directly from Git; it does not expect the composed active replacement to still equal its before-image. A new fixed browser bundle supplies only these exact source identities. Caller-provided historical routes or alternate source bundles are not accepted.

The new scope has an eleven-minute child deadline containing the ten-minute browser configuration, and the workflow has a thirty-minute job deadline. Packaging is bounded at 2,500 files, 96 MiB raw, 8 MiB individual and 15 MiB compressed, with one-day retention. A bounded incomplete fallback fails the workflow and cannot establish full backup acceptance. Existing modes retain their earlier source and execution bounds. The new automatic trigger is restricted to the named recovery branch and its explicit Notes/browser/shared dependency paths; manual dispatch remains available. Checkout credentials are not persisted and repository permission is read-only.

The helper requires exactly eight actual pass results on retry zero, all eight case records and screenshots, all staged ownership cleanup, eight retained synthetic final records, four unchanged successful host-response holds, the one observed genuine revision conflict and two exact raw browser downloads. Source postcheck success is labeled `source-verified` even when the earlier browser run failed; it never turns that failed behavioral run into a pass. Raw failed attempts and earlier source proposals remain preserved. Independent review and actual hosted acceptance remain pending.

The independent spec review identified an unnecessary escaped-dot ambiguity in the expected conflict observer's regular expression. The final observer uses exact string equality for the complete expected host error instead. The separate exact UI assertion remains required. The complete original spec/config/contract authority and six source files were preserved before this correction under `versions/spec-contract-v1`; none of its original claims were rewritten.
